{
  "title": "AI 2030",
  "subtitle": "I think the next four years of AI will shape my life more than anything else that happens in them: what work exists, what's dangerous, who has power, and what I should be learning right now.\n\nThis is my best guess at how it unfolds, written down so I can check it against reality every few months and change course early.",
  "span": "2026 → 2030",
  "summary": "My own AI 2027: where AI capabilities, money, politics and risk are heading, and what I should do about it.",
  "author": "Samartha",
  "started": "Oct 12, 2026",
  "updated": "Oct 12, 2026",
  "questions": [
    {
      "q": "What is this?",
      "a": "A scenario in the style of AI 2027: dated chapters about how AI might develop through 2030, with a dashboard that tracks capabilities, compute, money and public opinion as I scroll. The first chapter is reported from the news; the rest is my forecast."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does it matter to me?",
      "a": "I'm 19. Whatever AI does in the next four years decides which skills are worth building, which risks are real, and which doors stay open. I'd rather think it through now than be surprised."
    },
    {
      "q": "How sure am I?",
      "a": "Not very. I'm fairly confident about the direction and unsure about the speed. Every number after the first chapter is my estimate, and I'll revise them as things happen."
    },
    {
      "q": "What would change my mind?",
      "a": "AI agents stalling on long tasks for a year, revenue growth flattening, or a serious regulatory pause. Any of these would push my timeline back."
    }
  ],
